 DS\, INC.\, et al. v. BROCK \ncertiorari to the united states court of app
 eals for \nthe tenth circuit \nNo. 24–935. Argued March 25\, 2026—Deci
 ded May 28\, 2026 \nThe Federal Arbitration Act (FAA) requires courts to e
 nforce many pri-\nvate arbitration agreements\, but it also provides that 
 “nothing” in the \nlaw shall be used to compel arbitration in disputes
  involving the “con-\ntracts of employment” of any class of workers 
 “engaged in . . . interstate \ncommerce.” 9 U. S. C. § 1. This case p
 oses the question whether some-\none can qualify as a worker under the § 
 1 exemption if he never crosses \nstate lines and never interacts with veh
 icles that do. Flowers Foods\, \nInc.\, is a large producer of packaged ba
 ked goods with bakeries in 19 \nStates. To get its products to market\, th
 e company depends in part on \nfranchisees who buy the distribution rights
  to Flowers's products in \nspecifc geographic territories. Angelo Brock i
 s one such franchisee \nserving the Denver area\; he picks up Flowers's pr
 oducts from a ware-\nhouse in Colorado and delivers them to local stores\,
  all without leaving \nthe State. In 2022\, Brock sued Flowers in federal 
 district court alleg-\ning that the company had underpaid him and other di
 stributors in viola-\ntion of various federal and state laws. Flowers move
 d to compel arbi-\ntration\, arguing that the FAA generally requires court
 s to stay or \ndismiss cases when the parties have agreed to resolve their
  disputes by \narbit\nhttps://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/608us2r3
 7_i425.pdf
URL:https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/608us2r37_i425.pdf
