 KS CORP. v. ROYAL CARIBBEAN \nCRUISES\, LTD.\, et al. \ncertiorari to the 
 united states court of appeals for \nthe eleventh circuit \nNo. 24–983. 
 Argued February 23\, 2026—Decided May 21\, 2026 \nIn 1928\, the United S
 tates-based Havana Docks Corporation acquired from \nthe Cuban Government 
 a property interest in the development and op-\neration of docks at the Po
 rt of Havana. That property interest\, a usu-\nfructuary concession\, was 
 time-limited and set to expire in 2004. The \nCuban Government agreed that
 \, if it expropriated the docks before 2004\, \nit would compensate Havana
  Docks for the value of the works it had \nconstructed. After Fidel Castro
  seized power in 1959\, the new Cuban \nGovernment decreed that it would f
 orcibly take American-owned prop-\nerties and enterprises in Cuba and spec
 ifcally identifed Havana Docks. \nAs relevant here\, the Cuban Government 
 seized\, without compensation\, \nthe docks that Havana Docks had construc
 ted and its property interest \nin those docks. Havana Docks fled a claim 
 with the Foreign Claims \nSettlement Commission\, which certifed about $9 
 million in losses\, plus \nsix percent annual interest. Despite these cert
 ifed losses\, Havana \nDocks lacked any means to obtain compensation. That
  began to change \nin 1996\, when Congress enacted the Cuban Liberty and D
 emocratic Soli-\ndarity Act\, 22 U. S. C. § 6021 et seq .\, which creates
  a private right of \naction for United States nationals who own claims to
  “property w\nhttps://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/608us1r33_fcgj
 .pdf
URL:https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/608us1r33_fcgj.pdf
